.TH SILENCE-AUDIT 1 "2026-08-18" "realisateur" "bashified verb"
.SH NAME
silence-audit \- the ecosystem's null-discriminator: can a sensor tell nothing-there from could-not-look?
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B silence-audit
.br
.B silence-audit
--strict
.br
.B silence-audit
--self-test
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every other survey here answers "what is the state of the projects?". This
one answers a different question: can each mechanism distinguish
.IR nothing-there ,
.IR could-not-look ,
and
.I did-not-look
\(em or does it map all three onto one output symbol, which is silence?
.PP
Those are three distinct world-states. A sensor with one output symbol cannot
regulate a domain with three, and no quantity of added checks fixes that; you
must add output symbols. That is why this audits MECHANISMS rather than
projects.
.PP
The practical form of the rule: an exit code for BLIND that is never 0. A
guard that reports clean because it could not look is worse than one that
does not run, because it is believed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --strict
Exit 1 on any FLAG.
.TP
.B --self-test
Run the built-in fixtures. This carries its own witness rather than a
separate suite.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Audited; no --strict, or --strict and nothing flagged.
.TP
.B 1
--strict, and at least one mechanism cannot discriminate.
.TP
.B 3
BLIND \(em the set of mechanisms could not be read.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR closeout-lint (1),
.BR discipline (1)
